On 2006-05-25, Klaus Kassner wrote:
> Ohle Claussen wrote (schrieb, a �crit):
>> On 2006-05-24, Klaus Kassner wrote:
>> 
>>>                                                                  Once 
>>>your character really gets going, Slash'em becomes easier than Nethack, 
>>>because there are so many places to find stuff that you will get 
>>>whatever you need even with an unfavorable RNG.  
>
>> Oh, so this is even more so in Slash'em then in NetHack? Yet another reason
>> not to play it. I always find it peculiar how in the early game you are always
>> in fear of being killed because you're lacking a long list of important stuff, 
>> but when your character is finally buffed up you find tons of useful surplus 
>> stuff everywhere (especially Gehennom). 
>
> Actually, I like it this way.  And this would be a reason for me to play 
>   Slash'em rather than to avoid it.  In my opinion you *should* be able 
> to survive more easily by buffing up your character.  

Of course. But it's not (just) survivability I'm talking about, it's the
abundance of stuff that makes it unnecessary to improvise. Once you are at a
certain level, you can just go collect stuff, build a stash, enchant your
armor, alchemize your HP, and you're done. It takes some variety from the late
game. When you're lacking one thing or another, you have to come up with
different ways to protect yourself from certain dangers, making the game more
interesting.

>                                                      By playing well, 
> you should be able to get your survival probability close to one towards 
> the end.  It is o.k. to die inevitable deaths early on, but you should 
> not lose a character into which you have invested a lot to anything but 
> your own stupidity.  

True. Only that the amount of stupidity needed to die in the late game can be
quite high, leading to less thoughtful playing style. In the phase from the
castle to waking up Rodney (not that I've been there _that_ often...) , I'm 
always playing on autopilot, knowing that if I prepare for all the nasty
things that can happen, I'll be fine.

>                     So if the game becomes more deterministic and less 
> dependent on the RNG - for characters that have already survived the 
> first 50000 turns - that's good, not bad, IMHO.

It makes the late game more boring. Of course, if you lose a late game
character the frustration is immense, but that possibility can only make the
game more exciting.

